\n$$\n\nNote that ${-2,-3,-4}$ and ${9,10,11,12}$ each count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d1402c5-4acc-517b-957d-5e989e7f6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306098} {:answer/id #uuid "91361b35-1806-56a5-a47c-db4792c5aa9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91361b35-1806-56a5-a47c-db4792c5aa9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306099} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae666fef-4750-5c6e-be96-688bcbe5b75d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49131e72-c8a0-59f3-99af-aa1dff260098" :question/math-academy-id "q-181559" :question/problem "If $A={15,21,27,…,1\\,905},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n15,\\,21,\\,27,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6 \\\\  & =21-15 \\\\  & =27-21 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =15+(n-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =15+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={6n+9:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6N+9 & =1\\,905 \\\\ 6N & =1\\,896 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,896}{6} \\\\ N & =316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={6n+9:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 316}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $316$ elements, we must have $|A|=316.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae0a8adc-3c05-5f97-8b8e-71b9ffe16529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0a8adc-3c05-5f97-8b8e-71b9ffe16529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306102} :answer-field/id #uuid "1012c9f3-6e62-5d2d-a6e7-03327be3c804" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a92976e-8018-53a6-a819-bee2ab0a34ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-181562" :question/problem "If $A={2,10,18,…,100410},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,18,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =8 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2+8n-8 \\\\  & =8n-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8N-6 & =100\\,410 \\\\ 8N & =100\\,416 \\\\ N & =\\frac{100\\,416}{8} \\\\ N & =12\\,552\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={8n-6:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 12\\,552}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12\\,552$ elements, we must have $|A|=12\\,552.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81e53f55-af8d-51fb-a296-b63e29304373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81e53f55-af8d-51fb-a296-b63e29304373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306103} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f183342-6bfa-5336-98cc-06e7ffe19937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9e87e7b-6e84-5dd8-a2d8-4ac3f8e8903d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181563" :question/problem "If $A={-200,-193,-186,…,997},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-200,\\,-193,\\,-186,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =7 \\\\  & =-193-(-200) \\\\  & =-186-(-193) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-200+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-200+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7N-207 & =997 \\\\ 7N & =1\\,204 \\\\ N & =\\frac{1\\,204}{7} \\\\ N & =172\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={7n-207:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 172}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $172$ elements, we must have $|A|=172.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d84f4ab-176f-5135-9878-24d2edd65ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306106} {:answer/id #uuid "650eb2e2-6d72-5768-941a-777254debf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650eb2e2-6d72-5768-941a-777254debf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306107} :answer-field/id #uuid "f651eb26-4e2c-5118-b8bb-0d82665314d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4dbe27ab-40a5-53ce-97e8-2ca064c299b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-181564" :question/problem "If $A={-8,-5,-2,…,184},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-8,\\,-5,\\,-2,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =-5-(-8) \\\\  & =-2-(-5) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-8+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={3n-11:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3N-11 & =184 \\\\ 3N & =195 \\\\ N & =\\frac{195}{3} \\\\ N & =65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={3n-11:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 65}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $65$ elements, we must have $|A|=65.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6e95c6-7061-5fed-a14f-bc6af96b9b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1371" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6e95c6-7061-5fed-a14f-bc6af96b9b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1371" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306110} :answer-field/id #uuid "1333043c-4319-5cbb-9554-f2ba3278f0a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b782cd9-71cd-51a7-8679-e22d2b29be22" :question/math-academy-id "q-181565" :question/problem "If $A={-1\\,250,-1\\,235,-1\\,220,…,19\\,300},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n-1\\,250,\\,-1\\,235,\\,-1\\,220,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=15:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =15 \\\\  & =-1\\,235-(-1\\,250) \\\\  & =-1\\,220-(-1\\,235) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-1\\,250\\,+(n-1)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-1\\,250+15n-15 \\\\  & =15n-1\\,265.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15N-1\\,265 & =19\\,300 \\\\ 15N & =20\\,565 \\\\ N & =\\frac{20\\,565}{15} \\\\ N & =1\\,371\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={15n-1\\,265:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 1\\,371}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $1\\,371$ elements, we must have $|A|=1\\,371.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0206de21-5a60-5147-9a0f-f4caee187223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306113} {:answer/id #uuid "aad505d7-6c0a-5a37-a90a-ea5a533501e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aad505d7-6c0a-5a37-a90a-ea5a533501e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306114} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44c62b0-bf7a-5edb-8e92-4036af90ed11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd64efbb-bd14-5393-9b06-4438c059f63f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181575" :question/problem "If $A={5,15,45,135,…,7\\,971\\,615},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n5,\\,15,\\,45,\\,135,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{15} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=5({3}^{n-1})=\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{N} & =7\\,971\\,615 \\\\ {3}^{N} & =4\\,782\\,969 \\\\ N & ={log}_{3}⁡(4\\,782\\,969) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(4\\,782\\,969)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34cd758-ad00-511f-b62f-2288203de635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34cd758-ad00-511f-b62f-2288203de635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306117} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2196f8-084b-5bf3-a44f-def1c362bf61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7c6f1d2-1982-573c-ba1d-3b71c29477d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181576" :question/problem "If $A={2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb82449-80d9-5af1-a669-dc755c417dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306120} {:answer/id #uuid "02f706c8-8689-55bc-8338-58e4a6b8fa5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb82449-80d9-5af1-a669-dc755c417dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306120} :answer-field/id #uuid "12c8add7-ed5d-5b9c-a8aa-19bdb2e78490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e04b91c-9f76-5be0-b04c-887bec843436" :question/math-academy-id "q-181578" :question/problem "If $A={3,18,108,648,…,181\\,398\\,528},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,108,\\,648,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({6}^{n-1})=\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{N} & =181\\,398\\,528 \\\\ {6}^{N} & =362\\,797\\,056 \\\\ N & ={log}_{6}⁡(362\\,797\\,056) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(362\\,797\\,056)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e023ebb9-f5d9-503a-a18e-f06bd6ea2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e023ebb9-f5d9-503a-a18e-f06bd6ea2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306124} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac30c343-541b-548d-bae1-0ef841ae60de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22256f74-ecc6-5ea7-b1e4-d18dff2b6d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-181582" :question/problem "If $A={1,6,36,216,…,2\\,176\\,782\\,336},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,6,\\,36,\\,216,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {6}^{n-1}={6}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{N-1} & =2\\,176\\,782\\,336 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{6}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N-1 & =12 \\\\ N & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 13}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $13$ elements, we must have $|A|=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3ff0e5d-b30f-5eb8-ba83-b400e6c1c76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3ff0e5d-b30f-5eb8-ba83-b400e6c1c76f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306127} :answer-field/id #uuid "f290bfa4-b622-5f4d-8879-13eb7efca3a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b40818b3-dded-5ed0-bad5-7f30d36c8460" :question/math-academy-id "q-181583" :question/problem "If $A={1,11,121,1\\,331,…,214\\,358\\,881},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,11,\\,121,\\,1\\,331,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{11} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {11}^{n-1}={11}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{N-1} & =214\\,358\\,881 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{11}⁡(214\\,358\\,881) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(214\\,358\\,881)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\ N-1 & =8 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $9$ elements, we must have $|A|=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Set With Set Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b28d0eb-e93f-5a14-b5cd-297ff9e38876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306128} {:answer/id #uuid "646b3f76-235f-53e1-9aa6-63221e8e9ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646b3f76-235f-53e1-9aa6-63221e8e9ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306129} :answer-field/id #uuid "40a243b7-c7fa-5d87-992e-5412e6d11cca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cc7fe95-b089-501e-a708-9894e7faa612" :question/math-academy-id "q-181589" :question/problem "If $T={{},x,y,{v,x,{w}}},$ then $|T|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $T$ is the number of elements in $T.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $T:$\n\n$$\n{},\\,x,\\,y,\\,{v,x,{w}},\n$$\n\nNote that ${v,x,{w}}$ and ${}$ count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb35fcff-d9f0-5ae8-999e-7e8bbc0b0919" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Finite Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6608a19a-6473-5da0-9f14-4d574fc116d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306132} {:answer/id #uuid "98eac664-97e8-52a5-a867-5a9cf320cc44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306133} {:answer/id #uuid "a7870b0c-80d2-5966-a07f-f039834cbd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306134} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7af115-590e-57b9-9fbd-b9f4a6114e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306135} {:answer/id #uuid "639e0460-edbe-5107-8e55-6ee6371ac